<h1>Vertical Jump to Dunk, <em>By Height</em></h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📖 9 min read</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏀 Basketball</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">📊 By Height Chart</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Find your number below: how high you need to jump to dunk depends almost entirely on your height and arm length. A 6'4" player might need only a 20-inch vertical while a 5'9" player needs 36 or more. Here is the complete breakdown at every height — with arm length adjustments and what it takes to get there.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">

<div class="callout" style="background:var(--surface);border-color:var(--accent);">
<div class="callout-icon">🏀</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> To dunk a basketball, your hand must reach about <strong>6 inches above the 10-foot rim</strong> (roughly 10'6"). The vertical jump you need depends on your standing reach: a <strong>6'0" player</strong> with average arms needs about a <strong>26–28 inch</strong> vertical, a <strong>5'10" player</strong> needs about <strong>30–32 inches</strong>, and a <strong>6'3" player</strong> needs only about <strong>20–22 inches</strong>. Taller players and those with longer arms need less. The single biggest factor isn't your vertical — it's your standing reach.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Complete Dunk Height Chart</h2>
<p>To dunk, your hand needs to reach approximately <strong>6 inches above the 10-foot rim</strong> — meaning your fingertips must hit roughly 10 feet 6 inches. Your standing reach is your starting point. The difference between 10'6" and your standing reach is how high you need to jump.</p>
<p>The table below shows the required vertical for a two-foot standing jump and a one-foot running approach at each height, based on average arm proportions. Scroll down for the arm length adjustment table if your wingspan is unusually long or short.</p>
